# Local settings for ./gpb, gpb.ps1 and relay/deploy.ps1. Copy to gpb.conf and edit:
#
#   cp gpb.conf.example gpb.conf
#
# gpb.conf is gitignored, so your addresses and credentials never reach the repository.
#
# Plain KEY=value, one per line, no quotes and no inline comments. Values are taken literally to
# the end of the line, so a password may contain spaces, #, $ or a backslash without escaping.
# Lines starting with # are ignored, as are blank ones.


# =============================================================================== your relays
#
# One block per relay. The word in the middle of the key is a name YOU choose - letters and
# digits - and it is what you type on the command line:
#
#   ./gpb relay deploy sg          build, upload and install on the relay named sg
#   ./gpb relay logs sg            follow its log
#   ./gpb relay list               show every relay this file declares
#
# Only HOST is required. Every other field has the default shown in brackets.
#
#   RELAY_<NAME>_HOST       address or DNS name of the VPS                  (required)
#   RELAY_<NAME>_USER       SSH user                                        [root]
#   RELAY_<NAME>_PORT       SSH port - NOT the relay's UDP port             [22]
#   RELAY_<NAME>_KEY        private key file, ~ allowed                     [ssh decides]
#   RELAY_<NAME>_PASSWORD   password, if the host refuses key auth          [ssh asks you]
#   RELAY_<NAME>_SUDO_PASSWORD   password for sudo, when it differs         [the one above]
#   RELAY_<NAME>_LISTEN     UDP port relayd listens on, for players         [51820]
#
# Give either KEY or PASSWORD, not both - if both are set the key is tried first and the
# password is the fallback, which is what ssh does anyway.
#
# SUDO_PASSWORD is only for accounts that are not root. Installing writes to /usr/local/bin,
# /etc and systemd, so it needs root, and a deploy works this out on the far end rather than
# asking you to declare it:
#
#   the account IS root                 -> installs directly, one connection
#   sudo is configured NOPASSWD         -> installs under sudo, still one connection
#   sudo wants a password               -> a second connection carries it, because the first
#                                          one's stdin is the tarball and sudo -S reads its
#                                          password from stdin
#
# In that last case the password used is SUDO_PASSWORD, or PASSWORD when SUDO_PASSWORD is empty
# - a VPS you log into with a password normally has one password, not two. Set SUDO_PASSWORD on
# its own when you log in with a key but sudo still asks. Leave both empty and sudo prompts you
# on the terminal, which costs one typed password per deploy and nothing on disk.

RELAY_SG_HOST=203.0.113.10
RELAY_SG_USER=root
RELAY_SG_PORT=22
RELAY_SG_KEY=~/.ssh/id_ed25519
RELAY_SG_PASSWORD=
RELAY_SG_SUDO_PASSWORD=
RELAY_SG_LISTEN=51820

# A second relay needs nothing but a name and a host. Uncomment and edit:
#
# RELAY_HK_HOST=198.51.100.20
# RELAY_HK_KEY=~/.ssh/id_ed25519


# Relay used when a command needs one and you did not name it, as in `./gpb relay deploy`.
RELAY_DEFAULT=sg


# =============================================================================== notes
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS NOT
#
# Everything above is about reaching the VPS to deploy and operate it. It is read on your
# machine and nowhere else. The address a player's client sends packets to is a separate thing,
# declared in the files that ship to players:
#
#   the relay endpoint, <ip>:<LISTEN>  ->  profiles/<profile>.json, an entry in "relays"
#   the PSK and the relay to prefer    ->  client/config.json ("psk", "defaultRelayId")
#
# `./gpb relay deploy` prints both, ready to paste, when it finishes.
#
#
# USING ~/.ssh/config INSTEAD
#
# A name with no RELAY_<NAME>_HOST here is handed to ssh unchanged, so an alias from your own
# ~/.ssh/config, or a plain user@host, still works:
#
#   ./gpb relay deploy myalias
#   ./gpb relay deploy root@203.0.113.10
#
# Declaring the relay here is the simpler path; the alias is there if you already have one.
#
#
# BEFORE YOU FILL IN A PASSWORD
#
#   - It sits in plain text in a file. Anything that can read your files can read it: backup
#     software, a sync client, another program running as you. A key is better in every way -
#     it is never transmitted, it can carry a passphrase, and it is revoked per host by deleting
#     one line on the server. `./gpb relay setup` sets one up in about two minutes, and the last
#     password you type is the one that installs it.
#
#   - On Linux and macOS, restrict the file once you have:  chmod 600 gpb.conf
#
#   - Gitignored only protects you from git. It does not protect you from zipping the folder and
#     sending it to somebody.
#
# OpenSSH deliberately refuses to read a password from a flag, a file or an environment
# variable. What it does support is SSH_ASKPASS: it runs a helper program and reads the password
# from its output. That is what these scripts do with the value - write a small helper, hand it
# the password through the environment, and delete it afterwards, whatever happens.
#
# RELAY_PASSWORD (with no name in it) still works as a password for every relay that does not
# set its own. It is left in for older gpb.conf files; prefer the per-relay form.
